WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.486 --> 00:00:02.778
- Usually, in companies,
there's a notion of

00:00:02.778 --> 00:00:05.899
a hierarchy, a set of
objectives, a quarterly plan,

00:00:05.899 --> 00:00:07.251
and so we had none of that.

00:00:07.251 --> 00:00:08.716
There's always people smarter than you.

00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:12.190
There's always people who just
think more clearly than you.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.416
And if you can find such a
person, align yourself with them.

00:00:15.416 --> 00:00:17.398
As a general statement about society,

00:00:17.398 --> 00:00:19.366
we need to ask the harder questions,

00:00:19.366 --> 00:00:21.922
and we need to ask for stronger solutions.

00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:24.163
And one of the engineers, his name is Ron,

00:00:24.163 --> 00:00:27.990
pounds the table and said,
"That would be evil!"

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.723
And I'm like, oh, okay,
what just happened?

00:00:31.723 --> 00:00:34.383
- He's an American software
engineer, businessperson,

00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:36.737
and Executive Chairman of Alphabet.

00:00:36.737 --> 00:00:40.997
From 2001 to 2011, he
served as CEO of Google.

00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:44.965
In 2016, Forbes ranked him
as the 100th richest person

00:00:44.965 --> 00:00:48.820
in the world, with a fortune
of 10.8 billion dollars.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.586
He's Eric Schmidt, and here's my take

00:00:50.586 --> 00:00:52.589
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:52.589 --> 00:00:54.233
Rule #10 is my personal favorite,

00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:55.952
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:55.952 --> 00:00:57.328
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:57.328 --> 00:00:59.167
Also, as Eric is talking,
if he says something

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:00.749
that really resonates with you,

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:02.876
leave it in the comments,
and put it in quotes,

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:06.709
so that other people can
be inspired, as well!

00:01:08.923 --> 00:01:11.914
- Usually, in companies,
there's a notion of

00:01:11.914 --> 00:01:14.179
a hierarchy, a set of
objectives, a quarterly plan,

00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:15.930
sort of, we had none of that.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.390
Our theory was that the leadership,

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.824
and particularly the
bottoms-up leadership,

00:01:20.824 --> 00:01:23.046
really should be empowered
by what we were doing,

00:01:23.046 --> 00:01:24.863
and we'd see how far they get.

00:01:24.863 --> 00:01:26.264
We didn't have an 18-month strategy,

00:01:26.264 --> 00:01:27.970
we just kept iterating.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:29.388
So what I learned from this

00:01:29.388 --> 00:01:31.725
is that fast iteration is the key.

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:33.850
The most important thing
to do is, every week,

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.559
a new product, a new
idea, just keep pushing.

00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:38.827
You'd be amazed how much progress you make

00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:40.639
in three months or six months.

00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:42.726
- The background that you had, you know,

00:01:42.726 --> 00:01:46.893
working at Novell, working at
Bell Labs, working at Xerox.

00:01:48.297 --> 00:01:49.397
To that point in your career

00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:51.845
when you met Larry and Sergey,

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:56.237
I wonder what led you to
choose that opportunity next?

00:01:56.237 --> 00:02:00.404
What about them was so
different that made you say,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.311
okay, this is the next thing for me to do?

00:02:04.311 --> 00:02:06.146
- In my case, I'd been working at Novell,

00:02:06.146 --> 00:02:08.329
and I was invited to
visit Larry and Sergey.

00:02:08.329 --> 00:02:10.249
They invited me to their little office.

00:02:10.249 --> 00:02:12.001
They had all this food on the table,

00:02:12.001 --> 00:02:13.621
which had been prepared
by the Google chef,

00:02:13.621 --> 00:02:15.298
which I thought was rather odd.

00:02:15.298 --> 00:02:17.929
And they had my background
on the whiteboard,

00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:19.356
which they were projecting.

00:02:19.356 --> 00:02:22.130
And they proceeded to examine me,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.951
in terms of my ideas and the
technology we were building,

00:02:24.951 --> 00:02:27.435
which they disagreed with completely.

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:29.256
And I thought, well,
that's a very nice meeting.

00:02:29.256 --> 00:02:30.865
And I realized, as I left,

00:02:30.865 --> 00:02:34.010
and it was, curiously,
in an old Sun building,

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.414
that it was the most
interesting conversation

00:02:35.414 --> 00:02:37.081
I'd had in a decade.

00:02:38.152 --> 00:02:40.338
And that's why I joined.

00:02:40.338 --> 00:02:41.585
- Could it have gone another way?

00:02:41.585 --> 00:02:43.612
Was there a moment that
you looked at that,

00:02:43.612 --> 00:02:45.437
as you look back on that, and said,

00:02:45.437 --> 00:02:47.261
if this had happened, I
wouldn't have taken it,

00:02:47.261 --> 00:02:49.422
or if this had happened,
I wouldn't have gotten it.

00:02:49.422 --> 00:02:50.874
Are there moments like that?

00:02:50.874 --> 00:02:51.749
- I think, from my perspective,

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:54.111
is I'd already run a large organization.

00:02:54.111 --> 00:02:55.849
I wanted to work at a very small,

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:57.304
very, very smart organization,

00:02:57.304 --> 00:03:00.092
and I figured Google would be that.

00:03:00.092 --> 00:03:01.880
God knows it came out differently.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.017
But what I learned in this process is that

00:03:06.017 --> 00:03:08.006
there's always people smarter than you.

00:03:08.006 --> 00:03:11.353
There's always people who just
think more clearly than you.

00:03:11.353 --> 00:03:15.432
And if you can find such a
person, align yourself with them.

00:03:15.432 --> 00:03:17.951
In this case, these two young men,

00:03:17.951 --> 00:03:19.787
they're in their 20s, right?

00:03:19.787 --> 00:03:21.579
And they're awfully precocious.

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:23.700
And I haven't had that tough an argument

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.270
from such young men in a long time,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.130
and I thought, this is it.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.231
You know, you just knew.

00:03:30.231 --> 00:03:31.801
And these people are rare.

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:35.403
And we now know them as the
great founders in our industry,

00:03:35.403 --> 00:03:38.304
and I'm sure some of them
are here in the audience.

00:03:38.304 --> 00:03:40.502
You find such a person, work with them,

00:03:40.502 --> 00:03:43.584
work for them, manage
them, do whatever it takes.

00:03:43.584 --> 00:03:44.417
- [Interviewer] When
someone's pitching you,

00:03:44.417 --> 00:03:45.461
what don't you want to hear?

00:03:45.461 --> 00:03:46.600
Everyone asks what you do.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.184
What don't you want to hear?

00:03:48.184 --> 00:03:49.971
- I want to hear that everybody is focused

00:03:49.971 --> 00:03:54.168
on this new architecture,
and anybody who's coming in

00:03:54.168 --> 00:03:56.840
with plans based on the
earlier architectures,

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:57.673
you're not going to make it.

00:03:57.673 --> 00:04:00.879
You need to be on scalable
plans that solve real problems.

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:03.240
It's so easy to solve a local problem;

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.142
you need to dream bigger.

00:04:05.142 --> 00:04:07.622
Over and over again, we
hear ideas that interesting

00:04:07.622 --> 00:04:10.295
in a local context, but
they're not big enough.

00:04:10.295 --> 00:04:12.593
So dream bigger, right?

00:04:12.593 --> 00:04:14.551
As a general statement about society,

00:04:14.551 --> 00:04:16.371
we need to ask the harder questions,

00:04:16.371 --> 00:04:19.253
and we need to ask for stronger solutions.

00:04:19.253 --> 00:04:22.215
There is a global
emerging consensus around

00:04:22.215 --> 00:04:23.142
issues and so forth.

00:04:23.142 --> 00:04:25.505
Together, we're very strong.

00:04:25.505 --> 00:04:28.922
The people you hire to find the culture,

00:04:28.922 --> 00:04:30.713
whether you like it or not.

00:04:30.713 --> 00:04:32.158
So Larry and Sergey, when
they started the company,

00:04:32.158 --> 00:04:33.393
said we need to have really smart people,

00:04:33.393 --> 00:04:34.916
so they hired a rocket scientist,

00:04:34.916 --> 00:04:36.944
and they hired a medical doctor.

00:04:36.944 --> 00:04:38.687
And they hired a Stanford graduate

00:04:38.687 --> 00:04:40.634
who'd been a professional football player,

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:42.170
'cause they figured that they wanted

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:45.256
normal people who'd done
something exceptional.

00:04:45.256 --> 00:04:48.014
So in my case, I was a pilot,
and I had done other things,

00:04:48.014 --> 00:04:50.198
and they said, "Okay, you're good enough."

00:04:50.198 --> 00:04:51.378
You know, the CEO has to have something

00:04:51.378 --> 00:04:54.755
besides being a CEO that
makes them interesting.

00:04:54.755 --> 00:04:56.002
And everyone had something.

00:04:56.002 --> 00:04:56.915
I would go around, and I'd say,

00:04:56.915 --> 00:04:58.618
"Well, what's special about you?"

00:04:58.618 --> 00:05:00.179
It's like Sesame Street, you know?

00:05:00.179 --> 00:05:02.695
What's special about you,
and what's special about you?

00:05:02.695 --> 00:05:05.615
But there was always an interesting story.

00:05:05.615 --> 00:05:06.873
So another lesson I learned

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.639
is that you don't hire generic people.

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.623
You hire people who've
had some kind of stress

00:05:12.623 --> 00:05:14.518
or achievement or whatever.

00:05:14.518 --> 00:05:15.838
The best people to hire, by the way,

00:05:15.838 --> 00:05:18.179
are CFOs who've gone bankrupt.

00:05:18.179 --> 00:05:20.278
'Cause boy have they been through the war.

00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:23.180
And on the CFO side, and the finance side,

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:25.791
you want somebody who's
not going to happen again.

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:27.973
And you only go bankrupt once, by the way.

00:05:27.973 --> 00:05:30.448
- I'd like to ask you a general
question for all businesses.

00:05:30.448 --> 00:05:35.154
If you were coaching a
business class, or a company,

00:05:35.154 --> 00:05:37.611
how does a company remain nimble today?

00:05:37.611 --> 00:05:40.086
What do we have to do to remain nimble?

00:05:40.086 --> 00:05:42.649
- My problem with most
strategy conversations

00:05:42.649 --> 00:05:45.352
is that they're always inward-out.

00:05:45.352 --> 00:05:47.010
You know, we establish, well, first,

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.813
we're the best in the world,
'cause everybody thinks that.

00:05:48.813 --> 00:05:50.527
Which can't possibly be true.

00:05:50.527 --> 00:05:52.126
And second, we see that
all of our products

00:05:52.126 --> 00:05:53.641
are going to be successful, and therefore

00:05:53.641 --> 00:05:55.218
we'll be very successful in the future.

00:05:55.218 --> 00:05:57.895
And that's usually how
strategy is done in businesses.

00:05:57.895 --> 00:06:00.494
I prefer a completely different approach.

00:06:00.494 --> 00:06:03.302
Work very, very, very hard to figure out

00:06:03.302 --> 00:06:05.499
what the world's going to
look like in five years.

00:06:05.499 --> 00:06:06.625
What will people be doing?

00:06:06.625 --> 00:06:07.768
What will your customers want?

00:06:07.768 --> 00:06:09.781
Where costs will be.

00:06:09.781 --> 00:06:11.247
In our case, what will networks look like?

00:06:11.247 --> 00:06:12.478
How important will phones be?

00:06:12.478 --> 00:06:14.040
What will the apps look like?

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:14.873
Web services?

00:06:14.873 --> 00:06:16.844
And spend an awful lot of time on that,

00:06:16.844 --> 00:06:19.038
and then take a look at your offerings.

00:06:19.038 --> 00:06:20.226
What are the strongest ones,

00:06:20.226 --> 00:06:22.134
which are the ones that are
going to iterate quickly.

00:06:22.134 --> 00:06:23.277
You're probably gone do fine there,

00:06:23.277 --> 00:06:24.753
you understand these businesses.

00:06:24.753 --> 00:06:26.467
What about these others?

00:06:26.467 --> 00:06:28.519
If you think about, in the media business,

00:06:28.519 --> 00:06:30.358
there are media businesses
that are going very well,

00:06:30.358 --> 00:06:31.987
and others that are in great trouble.

00:06:31.987 --> 00:06:34.998
This was completely predicted
five years ago, right?

00:06:34.998 --> 00:06:37.278
So the leaders in the
ones that are in trouble

00:06:37.278 --> 00:06:41.214
should have, right, done a
five-year plan, and said,

00:06:41.214 --> 00:06:43.074
"Holy cow, what are we going to do?

00:06:43.074 --> 00:06:44.128
"What are the things we can do?"

00:06:44.128 --> 00:06:47.264
Or maybe we sell ourselves,
or we try something different.

00:06:47.264 --> 00:06:49.566
But almost all of these strategic shifts

00:06:49.566 --> 00:06:53.177
are predictable using my five year model.

00:06:53.177 --> 00:06:55.575
Almost nobody does a five-year plan.

00:06:55.575 --> 00:06:59.141
Most business do a one- to two-year plan.

00:06:59.141 --> 00:06:59.974
Especially in our space.

00:06:59.974 --> 00:07:01.689
But even in businesses, the only business

00:07:01.689 --> 00:07:05.541
that I discovered that does
a five-year or longer plan

00:07:05.541 --> 00:07:07.353
is the oil and gas industry.

00:07:07.353 --> 00:07:08.735
And the reason is because
they have to deploy

00:07:08.735 --> 00:07:10.563
so much capital and do all this planning.

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:11.896
But even there, they have trouble,

00:07:11.896 --> 00:07:13.859
because the prices go up
and down and so forth,

00:07:13.859 --> 00:07:15.575
so even there, it's difficult.

00:07:15.575 --> 00:07:17.761
- What would you tell a
founder they should do

00:07:17.761 --> 00:07:20.835
to protect and to hold onto their equity?

00:07:20.835 --> 00:07:24.235
What advice would you give them?

00:07:24.235 --> 00:07:27.159
- I can always tell when we're
dealing with a proper founder

00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:30.222
when we start with how are
they spending their money.

00:07:30.222 --> 00:07:32.239
And so whenever I go into a startup

00:07:32.239 --> 00:07:36.188
that has beautiful offices and
really nice chairs, I cringe.

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:38.558
'Cause that means that they
haven't quite figured out

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.069
that it's not their money.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:41.553
Unless it is their money, in which case

00:07:41.553 --> 00:07:43.896
they can waste it on that.

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:45.646
When I joined Google,

00:07:47.303 --> 00:07:50.904
I was given an office and I
had a little corner of a desk

00:07:50.904 --> 00:07:53.628
with four other engineers.

00:07:53.628 --> 00:07:56.449
Eventually I was given an 8x12 office,

00:07:56.449 --> 00:07:59.116
where my desk was a door with...

00:08:01.799 --> 00:08:02.691
Hobbyhorses, whatever they are.

00:08:02.691 --> 00:08:03.817
- [Interviewer] Like the
tabletop was your door?

00:08:03.817 --> 00:08:06.310
- The table top, literally a door.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:09.287
Which I've retained,
to try to remind myself

00:08:09.287 --> 00:08:11.039
of what it takes to be successful,

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:14.254
so my office today has that door in it.

00:08:14.254 --> 00:08:16.754
The great founders are frugal.

00:08:17.856 --> 00:08:19.991
They understand that the
money needs to be used

00:08:19.991 --> 00:08:22.164
precisely for certain areas.

00:08:22.164 --> 00:08:23.745
Many of the most successful founders

00:08:23.745 --> 00:08:26.078
begin with no salary at all.

00:08:27.477 --> 00:08:29.099
I have lots of stories
about Larry and Sergey,

00:08:29.099 --> 00:08:31.979
but let's just say that when I started,

00:08:31.979 --> 00:08:33.766
one of them was having
trouble with his car,

00:08:33.766 --> 00:08:37.594
so I had loan him my car
'cause his car broke down.

00:08:37.594 --> 00:08:41.878
I mean, that's the kind of
stuff we're talking about.

00:08:41.878 --> 00:08:44.974
So if you're not prepared to live that,

00:08:44.974 --> 00:08:48.339
you don't really understand
what being a founder's like.

00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:49.906
For me, I'd love to be a founder,

00:08:49.906 --> 00:08:51.850
I'd love to have a nice corner
office with a beautiful view,

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.599
and lots of secretaries, and all this art.

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:56.182
That's not how it works, right?

00:08:57.477 --> 00:09:00.299
And the great founders
always started that way.

00:09:00.299 --> 00:09:02.219
So that should give you some data.

00:09:02.219 --> 00:09:06.219
I don't agree that you
should narrow your focus.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:09.883
What I believe is that
you get the best outcome

00:09:09.883 --> 00:09:11.685
when you make the broadest appeal

00:09:11.685 --> 00:09:14.517
in terms of leadership and
excitement and so forth.

00:09:14.517 --> 00:09:18.394
And we worked very hard
to make that happen.

00:09:18.394 --> 00:09:19.264
But there were other things.

00:09:19.264 --> 00:09:20.702
For example, we would refuse

00:09:20.702 --> 00:09:22.684
to do exclusive deals with people.

00:09:22.684 --> 00:09:24.179
But I would explain to people,

00:09:24.179 --> 00:09:26.223
"Well, we don't really do exclusive deals,

00:09:26.223 --> 00:09:27.375
"but we only have the capacity

00:09:27.375 --> 00:09:30.030
"to work with one, and
it's going to be you."

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:30.863
Right?

00:09:31.765 --> 00:09:34.732
There are other ways of
achieving those kinds of things.

00:09:34.732 --> 00:09:37.229
So I'd be careful to conclude

00:09:37.229 --> 00:09:39.016
that you should do a small thing.

00:09:39.016 --> 00:09:42.533
All success starts from doing
one thing really, really well,

00:09:42.533 --> 00:09:45.125
but you'll recruit better
with a broader vision

00:09:45.125 --> 00:09:46.601
that's credible and
that you can articulate.

00:09:46.601 --> 00:09:48.893
But go back to that, selling the dream.

00:09:48.893 --> 00:09:51.305
What you do is selling a dream.

00:09:51.305 --> 00:09:52.494
If you can't sell the dream,

00:09:52.494 --> 00:09:54.231
then you're not going to be successful.

00:09:54.231 --> 00:09:55.857
I've received so many
different kinds of advice

00:09:55.857 --> 00:09:57.132
I don't know where to start.

00:09:57.132 --> 00:09:59.888
One that comes to mind is to have a coach.

00:09:59.888 --> 00:10:01.361
Our board member in 2002, John Doerr,

00:10:01.361 --> 00:10:02.666
said, "You need a coach."

00:10:02.666 --> 00:10:04.059
And I said, well, I don't need a coach,

00:10:04.059 --> 00:10:05.814
I'm an established CEO,
why would I need a coach?

00:10:05.814 --> 00:10:06.698
Is something wrong?

00:10:06.698 --> 00:10:07.740
He said, "No, no, no, you need a coach.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.743
"Everybody needs a coach."

00:10:09.743 --> 00:10:11.511
So Bill Campbell became my coach,

00:10:11.511 --> 00:10:14.213
and it served Google very well.

00:10:14.213 --> 00:10:17.717
Every famous athlete,
every famous performer,

00:10:17.717 --> 00:10:19.815
has somebody who's a coach.

00:10:19.815 --> 00:10:22.225
Somebody who can watch
what they're doing and say,

00:10:22.225 --> 00:10:23.496
"Is that what you really meant?

00:10:23.496 --> 00:10:24.424
"Did you really do that?"

00:10:24.424 --> 00:10:25.387
And give them perspective.

00:10:25.387 --> 00:10:27.487
The one thing people are never good at

00:10:27.487 --> 00:10:29.887
is seeing themselves as others see them.

00:10:29.887 --> 00:10:31.706
A coach really, really helps.

00:10:31.706 --> 00:10:33.605
So one of the ideas that
we had a few years ago

00:10:33.605 --> 00:10:35.715
was that we would be better off

00:10:35.715 --> 00:10:38.657
with more and more
people in our ecosystem.

00:10:38.657 --> 00:10:40.015
And the best way to do that

00:10:40.015 --> 00:10:42.809
is to essentially help
entrepreneurs be successful.

00:10:42.809 --> 00:10:44.550
It doesn't mean they're captive to us,

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.483
they're still going to
use competitor products,

00:10:46.483 --> 00:10:49.423
and competitor ad systems
and competitor platforms.

00:10:49.423 --> 00:10:51.701
But it seems one of the things that people

00:10:51.701 --> 00:10:54.294
don't understand when they view business

00:10:54.294 --> 00:10:57.878
is they assume that business
is, how to describe it,

00:10:57.878 --> 00:10:59.425
it's a zero sum game.

00:10:59.425 --> 00:11:02.186
That if company A wins, company B loses.

00:11:02.186 --> 00:11:04.848
I'll tell you that when Yahoo was young

00:11:04.848 --> 00:11:05.955
and Terry Semel was running it,

00:11:05.955 --> 00:11:08.714
whenever he and his
company would win a deal,

00:11:08.714 --> 00:11:10.910
I would call him to congratulate him.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.241
And whenever we would win a deal,

00:11:12.241 --> 00:11:14.248
he would call me to congratulate me.

00:11:14.248 --> 00:11:17.090
And by the way, we were tough competitors.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.057
Why would we do that?

00:11:19.057 --> 00:11:21.242
Because we both understood
that we were building

00:11:21.242 --> 00:11:23.210
a larger market.

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:26.449
So you can understand our
goal as philanthropic,

00:11:26.449 --> 00:11:28.644
you can understand it as making
the world a better place,

00:11:28.644 --> 00:11:30.796
but you can also understand
it as self-interested,

00:11:30.796 --> 00:11:34.011
in that building more people
who understand the concepts

00:11:34.011 --> 00:11:36.476
of network computing and
platforms and so forth

00:11:36.476 --> 00:11:38.905
benefits everyone, including Google.

00:11:38.905 --> 00:11:42.365
20% time was a rule invented,
again, when I started there,

00:11:42.365 --> 00:11:43.612
there were two actually interesting.

00:11:43.612 --> 00:11:45.820
One was "don't be evil", and
the second one was "20% time."

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:49.041
"Don't be evil" I thought was a joke.

00:11:49.041 --> 00:11:50.670
'Cause I mean, nobody here is evil.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.503
I don't want to be evil,
you don't want to be evil.

00:11:52.503 --> 00:11:55.208
And so I was sitting in the
original conference room

00:11:55.208 --> 00:11:58.902
in this small building where we started,

00:11:58.902 --> 00:12:02.600
and there's conversation
about ad targeting

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.449
and a particular ad click,
and one of the engineers,

00:12:05.449 --> 00:12:07.803
his name is Ron, pounds the table,

00:12:07.803 --> 00:12:10.945
and said, "That would be evil!"

00:12:10.945 --> 00:12:14.511
And I'm like, oh, okay,
what just happened?

00:12:14.511 --> 00:12:17.761
And the whole mood of the room changes,

00:12:20.255 --> 00:12:21.866
and there's this huge debate over

00:12:21.866 --> 00:12:24.198
whether that's evil or not.

00:12:24.198 --> 00:12:27.643
And ultimately, they didn't do the change.

00:12:27.643 --> 00:12:29.682
And so I thought, what's
the analogy there?

00:12:29.682 --> 00:12:33.521
And the analogy was, it's
the kanban system, right?

00:12:33.521 --> 00:12:35.527
In Japan, the notion that any employee

00:12:35.527 --> 00:12:39.090
can stop the line if they
see a poor quality thing.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.273
And it works, 'cause of shared values.

00:12:41.273 --> 00:12:44.262
Strong shared values, a
strong buy into principles,

00:12:44.262 --> 00:12:46.065
takes you far.

00:12:46.065 --> 00:12:47.340
I think that helped us.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:50.471
And that, I think it may
or may not be true today

00:12:50.471 --> 00:12:52.232
that the company has that feeling,

00:12:52.232 --> 00:12:55.732
but certainly its history is clearly that.

00:12:57.718 --> 00:12:59.315
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:12:59.315 --> 00:13:02.195
I made this video because
Sam Borsten asked me to.

00:13:02.195 --> 00:13:03.431
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:03.431 --> 00:13:05.002
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:05.002 --> 00:13:06.744
leave it down in the comments below,

00:13:06.744 --> 00:13:07.991
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:07.991 --> 00:13:09.440
I'd also love to know
which of the 10 rules

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:11.063
had the biggest impact on you, and why.

00:13:11.063 --> 00:13:12.083
What did you learn from this?

00:13:12.083 --> 00:13:14.218
What are you going to change
in your business or your life

00:13:14.218 --> 00:13:15.981
after watching this video?

00:13:15.981 --> 00:13:18.478
Leave it in the comments, and
I will join in the discussion.

00:13:18.478 --> 00:13:21.000
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Jim Firaben.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.679
Jim, thank you so much for buying my book,

00:13:22.679 --> 00:13:24.528
it really means a lot to me.

00:13:24.528 --> 00:13:25.361
For those of those you watching,

00:13:25.361 --> 00:13:27.419
if you want your chance at a
shout out in a future video,

00:13:27.419 --> 00:13:29.519
make sure to pick up a copy of the book

00:13:29.519 --> 00:13:31.567
and email in your receipt
so we can keep track.

00:13:31.567 --> 00:13:33.441
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:33.441 --> 00:13:36.492
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is!

00:13:36.492 --> 00:13:38.325
And I'll see you soon.

00:13:43.753 --> 00:13:45.353
- I learned a lot from our founders.

00:13:45.353 --> 00:13:46.940
And one of the things I learned was

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:49.972
ask the impossible and really dream big.

00:13:49.972 --> 00:13:52.405
What you'll learn about
people is if you paint

00:13:52.405 --> 00:13:55.129
a very broad vision, you attract people

00:13:55.129 --> 00:13:56.772
who really want to make it happen,

00:13:56.772 --> 00:13:58.343
'cause they're dreamers, too.

00:13:58.343 --> 00:13:59.545
So you can inspire people,

00:13:59.545 --> 00:14:02.338
and with that inspiration,
you get the smartest people,

00:14:02.338 --> 00:14:04.052
and they deal with a lot of stuff.

00:14:04.052 --> 00:14:06.172
- But can an organization like Google,

00:14:06.172 --> 00:14:09.131
which espouses the idea
that you're going to give

00:14:09.131 --> 00:14:12.482
people in the organization
15 or 20% of their time

00:14:12.482 --> 00:14:15.200
to do almost anything they
want, in terms of innovation,

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.492
can that system work in smaller companies?

00:14:17.492 --> 00:14:19.134
Can that system work in any company?

00:14:19.134 --> 00:14:20.755
Is that a leadership tenant that can work

00:14:20.755 --> 00:14:22.507
across a broad range of companies?

00:14:22.507 --> 00:14:24.740
- Well, in fact, it worked at
Google when Google was tiny.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.150
So the answer is yes, it
does work in small companies.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.711
Remember, we give this to engineers,

00:14:28.711 --> 00:14:30.738
and engineers are not that clever.

00:14:30.738 --> 00:14:33.699
If they're going to work on
something in their 20% time,

00:14:33.699 --> 00:14:37.038
it's going to be something
that's pretty related

00:14:37.038 --> 00:14:39.042
to their training and their knowledge.

00:14:39.042 --> 00:14:41.250
And that's why it's so powerful.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:43.517
Very, very few people sort
of get off the reservation

00:14:43.517 --> 00:14:45.105
and do something completely different

00:14:45.105 --> 00:14:47.341
from technology and so
forth, and if they did,

00:14:47.341 --> 00:14:48.769
we would encourage it.

00:14:48.769 --> 00:14:51.242
- Has something manifested you can recall

00:14:51.242 --> 00:14:54.290
come from just that kind of incubation?

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:56.581
- Virtually all of the
interesting apps now

00:14:56.581 --> 00:14:59.843
came from a combination of an acquisition

00:14:59.843 --> 00:15:01.416
and a 20% time.

00:15:01.416 --> 00:15:03.382
If you look at Maps, if
you look at Google Earth,

00:15:03.382 --> 00:15:05.425
if you look at Chrome and Android,

00:15:05.425 --> 00:15:08.965
all of them came from
somebody trying something,

00:15:08.965 --> 00:15:11.294
identifying something,
and then we would say,

00:15:11.294 --> 00:15:14.606
we have all these 20% timers
but we need a real group!

00:15:14.606 --> 00:15:15.780
And the fastest way to do that was to

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:18.660
create a real group or buy a company.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:21.335
I want to offer a cautionary tale.

00:15:21.335 --> 00:15:22.648
And again, having both read your book

00:15:22.648 --> 00:15:27.256
and also the notes that you
kindly sent me before this,

00:15:27.256 --> 00:15:29.322
if you don't understand the subtlety here,

00:15:29.322 --> 00:15:31.854
you would conclude that the
correct thing to do is to grow

00:15:31.854 --> 00:15:34.351
everything as fast as you
possibly can, everywhere.

00:15:34.351 --> 00:15:35.250
Right?

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:36.627
So let's just make as many engineers,

00:15:36.627 --> 00:15:39.750
as many salespeople, as many
products, and so on and so on.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.594
That completely does not work.

00:15:42.594 --> 00:15:46.244
And it doesn't work because
no product shall ship

00:15:46.244 --> 00:15:48.728
before it actually works.

00:15:48.728 --> 00:15:50.180
Right?

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:53.251
And the way you build great products

00:15:53.251 --> 00:15:56.178
is you have small teams
with strong leaders,

00:15:56.178 --> 00:15:59.415
who obsess over trade-offs,
and they push things off,

00:15:59.415 --> 00:16:00.901
and they say, "We've got to get it done,"

00:16:00.901 --> 00:16:02.620
and they put a lot of
pressure on the team.

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:04.309
They work all night, and
they produce a product

00:16:04.309 --> 00:16:06.226
that just barely works.

00:16:07.103 --> 00:16:09.244
The original, I'll use
non-Google examples,

00:16:09.244 --> 00:16:11.249
the original iPod just barely worked.

00:16:11.249 --> 00:16:12.728
Look at what it became.

00:16:12.728 --> 00:16:15.535
Try, people, remember the original iPhone.

00:16:15.535 --> 00:16:17.179
No apps, right?

00:16:17.179 --> 00:16:18.523
Everyone's forgotten that.

00:16:18.523 --> 00:16:19.976
Just barely worked.

00:16:19.976 --> 00:16:22.713
But it just was just the right combination

00:16:22.713 --> 00:16:26.260
to create an enormous
franchise that's now 70%

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.795
of the revenue of the world's
most valuable company.

00:16:28.795 --> 00:16:32.128
I was talking to Travis Kalanick, we are

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.468
major investors inside of Uber,

00:16:35.468 --> 00:16:37.293
so we have good relationships with Uber.

00:16:37.293 --> 00:16:42.034
And his description of Uber
was that he understood scaling,

00:16:42.034 --> 00:16:45.342
but the product, the app, wasn't ready.

00:16:45.342 --> 00:16:48.113
So you have to sort of
have judgment about when

00:16:48.113 --> 00:16:51.269
the product is ready to scale against it.

00:16:51.269 --> 00:16:55.335
And so we would debate
this over and over again.

00:16:55.335 --> 00:16:57.235
And so Larry and Sergey
would play tricks on me.

00:16:57.235 --> 00:17:00.199
So a typical example
would be, I would say,

00:17:00.199 --> 00:17:02.657
we're not strong enough
to take on Microsoft.

00:17:02.657 --> 00:17:05.504
And they wanted to do an
operating system and a browser.

00:17:05.504 --> 00:17:07.315
And I said, there's no
scenario where we'll ever do

00:17:07.315 --> 00:17:09.826
an operating system and a browser.

00:17:09.826 --> 00:17:10.728
'Cause Microsoft will kill us,

00:17:10.728 --> 00:17:11.891
and I don't want to get killed.

00:17:11.891 --> 00:17:13.894
We're a small company, and
yes, yes, you're very smart,

00:17:13.894 --> 00:17:15.238
and yes, yes, we're full of smart people,

00:17:15.238 --> 00:17:16.593
and yes, yes, we have lots of revenue;

00:17:16.593 --> 00:17:17.426
these guys can kill us.

00:17:17.426 --> 00:17:19.870
'Cause I'd been previously
killed in previous jobs.

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:22.678
And so they hired somebody to improve

00:17:22.678 --> 00:17:24.928
the performance of Firefox.

00:17:25.858 --> 00:17:27.993
Six months later, I'm
called down to one office,

00:17:27.993 --> 00:17:29.735
and this person who's supposed to improve

00:17:29.735 --> 00:17:34.359
the performance of Firefox
has managed to invent Chrome.

00:17:34.359 --> 00:17:35.192
Shocking.

00:17:35.192 --> 00:17:36.513
(audience laughter)

00:17:36.513 --> 00:17:38.107
And I say, well how long
have you been doing this?

00:17:38.107 --> 00:17:39.599
"Well, I've been doing it on the side."

00:17:39.599 --> 00:17:40.912
No you haven't, you've done it full-time.

00:17:40.912 --> 00:17:42.493
"Well, yeah, you know, I
had three other people."

00:17:42.493 --> 00:17:43.419
Okay, well, how many
more people do you have?

00:17:43.419 --> 00:17:44.969
"Oh, 10 more people."

00:17:44.969 --> 00:17:46.976
Well, okay, did Larry and
Sergey know about this?

00:17:46.976 --> 00:17:49.671
"Larry and Sergey encouraged it."

00:17:49.671 --> 00:17:50.651
Those assholes, right?

00:17:50.651 --> 00:17:51.671
(loud laughter)

00:17:51.671 --> 00:17:52.504
I knew it!

00:17:52.504 --> 00:17:55.864
I knew they were going around me, right?

00:17:55.864 --> 00:17:56.697
Okay?

00:18:02.223 --> 00:18:04.267
Then I said, well, we can't
do an operating system,

00:18:04.267 --> 00:18:06.719
so they bought this
company called Android,

00:18:06.719 --> 00:18:08.401
and they said, "Yeah,
it's just for smartphones,

00:18:08.401 --> 00:18:12.653
"it's software for smartphones,
don't worry, Eric."

00:18:12.653 --> 00:18:16.820
Today Android is 1.4 billion
plus operating systems used,

00:18:17.675 --> 00:18:19.644
largest number of operating
systems in the world,

00:18:19.644 --> 00:18:22.138
Chrome is the largest and
most successful browser.

00:18:22.138 --> 00:18:24.656
So, maybe the lesson to
learn in management is

00:18:24.656 --> 00:18:26.522
I'm just wrong all the time.

00:18:26.522 --> 00:18:30.108
But the secret was, you sort
of, you have to have judgment

00:18:30.108 --> 00:18:33.063
as to when these things can scale, right?

00:18:33.063 --> 00:18:36.941
And I can give you plenty
of negative examples.

00:18:36.941 --> 00:18:38.532
An obvious one is Wave.

00:18:38.532 --> 00:18:40.837
It was a complicated email product

00:18:40.837 --> 00:18:43.979
that we launched to great fanfare,

00:18:43.979 --> 00:18:45.672
and we watched its adoption rate.

00:18:45.672 --> 00:18:48.288
Marissa had this rule,
which is a good rule,

00:18:48.288 --> 00:18:49.813
that you cannot, at
Google, you cannot tell

00:18:49.813 --> 00:18:51.951
how successful a product is
until the first six months.

00:18:51.951 --> 00:18:54.074
Because what happens is you
get this adoption cycle,

00:18:54.074 --> 00:18:54.998
and everyone loves the product,

00:18:54.998 --> 00:18:55.831
'cause everyone tests it,

00:18:55.831 --> 00:18:57.652
and then you watch what happens.

00:18:57.652 --> 00:18:58.916
And the great products, you know,

00:18:58.916 --> 00:19:00.617
they're bumpy, but they go up.

00:19:00.617 --> 00:19:03.091
Well, this is what it
looked like, like this.

00:19:03.091 --> 00:19:05.203
So it took Eric, your friendly CEO,

00:19:05.203 --> 00:19:07.578
18 months of going down, straight down,

00:19:07.578 --> 00:19:10.267
before the project was canceled.

00:19:10.267 --> 00:19:13.071
So again, lack of due diligence lasts.

00:19:13.071 --> 00:19:14.969
The reason I'm answering your question

00:19:14.969 --> 00:19:18.532
in the Blitz growth argument by saying

00:19:18.532 --> 00:19:21.810
you've got to have the
products that you can scale.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:23.896
If you have the products that can scale,

00:19:23.896 --> 00:19:26.835
the thing that's new is
you can scale very quickly.

00:19:26.835 --> 00:19:28.626
The Uber example.

00:19:28.626 --> 00:19:31.733
Once you have an app and a business model

00:19:31.733 --> 00:19:33.084
that more or less works,

00:19:33.084 --> 00:19:35.982
there's no reason why you
shouldn't go global and wide.

00:19:35.982 --> 00:19:40.050
Successful companies are a set of things.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:44.898
So Google was and is this
remarkable technical platform;

00:19:44.898 --> 00:19:47.490
it also was a new way
of doing advertising.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:49.831
ROI-based advertising.

00:19:49.831 --> 00:19:51.607
And if you go back to your career

00:19:51.607 --> 00:19:52.772
and look at what you've
done in sales for us,

00:19:52.772 --> 00:19:55.260
what you did at Oracle, what
I did at these other jobs,

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:56.553
they all had the property that you had

00:19:56.553 --> 00:19:58.576
an interesting platform
that the technologists saw,

00:19:58.576 --> 00:20:01.254
and you also had a monetization model.

00:20:01.254 --> 00:20:04.075
The next generation of that is going to be

00:20:04.075 --> 00:20:06.921
applications monetization,
web services monetization,

00:20:06.921 --> 00:20:09.042
the way these developers,
this new generation

00:20:09.042 --> 00:20:10.446
can make money.

00:20:10.446 --> 00:20:13.350
'Cause ultimately, if they
want to be very successful,

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:15.906
there has to be revenue
coming in to focus everything.

00:20:15.906 --> 00:20:19.322
So I think, for me, the
fact that we were able

00:20:19.322 --> 00:20:23.706
to combine those was
both the hardest thing,

00:20:23.706 --> 00:20:25.565
but also the most powerful thing.

00:20:25.565 --> 00:20:27.373
Because when the two work together,

00:20:27.373 --> 00:20:28.726
it happens very, very quickly.

